    "custom_product_tabs_zy6jqD": {
      "type": "custom-product-tabs",
      "blocks": {
        "tab_item_BC78mP": {
          "type": "tab-item",
          "settings": {
            "title": "What is an electric bike?",
            "t_icon": "",
            "t_color": "#ffffff",
            "content": "<p>An electric bike, or e-bike, is a bicycle equipped with a rechargeable battery, electric motor, and controls that provide assistance while you ride. Depending on the model and local rules, that assistance may come through pedal assist, a throttle, or both. You can still pedal it like a regular bicycle when the motor is not in use.</p>"
          }
        },
        "tab_item_fxwnPf": {
          "type": "tab-item",
          "settings": {
            "title": "How do electric bikes work?",
            "t_icon": "",
            "t_color": "#ffffff",
            "content": "<p>An e-bike's battery supplies power to the motor through a controller. Pedal-assist models add power when you pedal, while throttle-equipped models can deliver power from a handlebar control. The rider selects the assistance level; depending on the system, assistance stops when you stop pedaling, release the throttle, brake, or reach the configured limit.</p>"
          }
        },
        "tab_item_RyhTyF": {
          "type": "tab-item",
          "settings": {
            "title": "How much does an electric bike cost?",
            "t_icon": "",
            "t_color": "#ffffff",
            "content": "<p>Electric bikes can cost from under $1,000 to several thousand dollars. Price is mainly affected by battery capacity, motor system, frame, brakes, and suspension. Macfox models shown on this page currently start at $1,099; check the product cards above for current prices, promotions, and battery options.</p>"
          }
        },
        "tab_item_ixpqJE": {
          "type": "tab-item",
          "settings": {
            "title": "How fast do electric bikes go?",
            "t_icon": "",
            "t_color": "#ffffff",
            "content": "<p>In the common U.S. three-class system, Class 1 and Class 2 motor assistance ends at 20 mph, while Class 3 pedal assistance ends at 28 mph. Macfox models on this page list a 20 mph top speed. Applicable rules and permitted riding locations vary by state and locality.</p>"
          }
        },
        "tab_item_ttdNgF": {
          "type": "tab-item",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Do you need a license for an electric bike?",
            "t_icon": "",
            "t_color": "#ffffff",
            "content": "<p>Many standard e-bikes do not require a driver's license, registration, or insurance, but the rule is not uniform across the United States. Requirements can depend on the state, locality, bike classification, rider age, and where you ride. Check current state and local regulations before riding.</p>"
          }
        },
        "tab_item_4iY88k": {
          "type": "tab-item",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Are electric bikes street legal?",
            "t_icon": "",
            "t_color": "#ffffff",
            "content": "<p>E-bikes are street legal only when the bike and its use comply with applicable state and local rules. Classification, assisted speed, throttle use, rider age, helmet requirements, and permitted roads or paths can all matter. A bike allowed on a road may still be restricted on sidewalks, trails, or multi-use paths.</p>"
          }
        },
        "tab_item_jnLb8K": {
          "type": "tab-item",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Do you have to pedal an electric bike?",
            "t_icon": "",
            "t_color": "#ffffff",
            "content": "<p>Not always. Pedal-assist e-bikes require pedaling for the motor to assist, while throttle-equipped e-bikes can provide power without pedaling where throttle use is permitted. Macfox models on this page include a thumb throttle and can also be pedaled.</p>"
          }
        },
        "tab_item_bhaLTB": {
          "type": "tab-item",
          "settings": {
            "title": "How far can an electric bike go on one charge?",
            "t_icon": "",
            "t_color": "#ffffff",
            "content": "<p>Range changes with battery configuration, assist level, rider and cargo weight, terrain, temperature, tire pressure, and stop-and-go riding. Macfox models on this page list 25-40 miles with a single battery and up to 56-80 miles with available dual-battery setups on select models. Actual range will vary.<br/></p>"
          }
        },
        "tab_item_xyBxkW": {
          "type": "tab-item",
          "settings": {
            "title": "How long does it take to charge an electric bike?",
            "t_icon": "",
            "t_color": "#ffffff",
            "content": "<p>Macfox models on this page list charge times of about 5-7 hours, depending on the model and battery. Use only the supplied or manufacturer-recommended charger, stay nearby while charging, and unplug the charger when charging is complete.</p>"
          }
        },
        "tab_item_z3gTHG": {
          "type": "tab-item",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Can you ride an electric bike in the rain?",
            "t_icon": "",
            "t_color": "#ffffff",
            "content": "<p>Some water-resistant e-bikes can be ridden in light rain if the model instructions permit it, but water-resistant does not mean waterproof. Check the bike's stated protection and manual, avoid deep water, submersion, and pressure washing, and make sure electrical components are dry before charging.</p>"
          }
        },
        "tab_item_qVjYkz": {
          "type": "tab-item",
          "settings": {
            "title": "How much do electric bikes weigh?",
            "t_icon": "",
            "t_color": "#ffffff",
            "content": "<p>Electric bikes are usually heavier than conventional bicycles because of the motor, battery, frame, and wider tires. Current Macfox models on this page weigh about 65-87 lb. Check the exact model specifications if you need to lift the bike, carry it upstairs, or use a vehicle rack.</p>"
          }
        },
        "tab_item_xAwHBR": {
          "type": "tab-item",
          "settings": {
            "title": "Are electric bikes safe?",
            "t_icon": "",
            "t_color": "#ffffff",
            "content": "<p>Electric bikes can be safe when correctly sized, maintained, and ridden within local rules. Wear a bicycle helmet, check the brakes, tires, throttle, lights, battery, and frame before riding, and use only approved batteries and chargers. Do not make unauthorized changes to the battery or electronic control system.</p>"
          }
        }
      },
